## Test files written this session

- `.worktrees/task-detail-and-dates/test/task-priority.test.ts` — 12 cases across
  7 `it`/`it.each` blocks, all deriving from PRD AC-4:
  - three accepted enum values round-trip through `POST /api/tasks` and are
    present in the stored row, not merely echoed in the response;
  - an omitted priority and an explicit `null` both persist as "not set"
    (the PRD Decisions Log: `NULL` means not set and sorts as `normal`);
  - five out-of-enum inputs (`"urgent"`, the legacy integer `3`, a boolean,
    an empty string, and the wrong-cased `"High"`) are each rejected with 400
    **and** leave the table empty — the "nothing is written" half of AC-4;
  - a direct Drizzle insert of `'urgent'` is rejected by the database itself,
    proving the SQL CHECK exists rather than only the TypeScript union;
  - the three enum values and `NULL` are each accepted on a direct insert, so
    the CHECK is not merely rejecting everything.

## Why this suite is legitimately RED before implementation

Against `HEAD` (`09931ba`) the failures are the two that matter, and they fail
for the right reason rather than by construction:

- `POST /api/tasks` forwards `input.priority ?? null` straight into the insert
  (`src/worker/routes/tasks.ts:74`) with no validation, so every out-of-enum
  case returns 201 today instead of 400.
- `tasks.priority` is `integer("priority")` with no CHECK
  (`src/worker/db/schema.ts:158`), so the direct-write case inserts happily
  instead of throwing.

No test asserts on an invented method name, a private field, or a call count;
every assertion is on an observable HTTP status, a response field, or a stored
row. No mocks are used at all — the suite runs the real Worker inside workerd
against an ephemeral D1 built from `migrations/`, matching the idiom already
established by `test/tasks.test.ts`.

